this is basically a follow up to the ealier posting I made about
a problem with rnd.c.  It seems I assesed the situation in correcly.
the problem was not with the register declarations in the rnd functions,
the problem is that occasionally rnd() is getting passed a 0, this causes
a divide by 0 which has serious consequences on the 386, resulting in the
illegal instruction problem I have been having in nethack. (this is all
being done on Xenix 386 2.3.2 withthe 2.3.0 development system, but I have
tried the offending code under ISC unix and it has the same problem).
I looked at the places where rnd is being passed a variable and it looks like
it is quite easy to get a 0 passed into this routine.  Is this only a problem 
with 80x86 line or does anyone else experience this problem????  The soloution
is to either modify the calling locations so 0 input is not possible or to
modify the rnd funtions to handle a 0 input.
 
Can someone make sure that the development team gets this?


